584
00:55:11,001 --> 00:55:19,850
Uh, I am the chair of the, uh, of a committee that advises our, excuse me, our select board on noise issues and, um,

585
00:55:21,623 --> 00:55:34,931
After about three and a half years of discussion, we brought forward an article to town meeting this past fall, part of which was fairly conventional, part of which was not.

586
00:55:35,131 --> 00:55:49,040
There are a number of towns in this area, Newton, Cambridge, Brookline, Lincoln, some others, that have already put in place some form of restriction on the use of gasoline blowers because of the noise and the

587
00:55:50,524 --> 00:55:54,065
the really remarkable level of emissions and the toxicity of emissions.

588
00:55:55,165 --> 00:56:01,326
Most of those, the most common thing is to simply ban them in the summer, where there's really no reason for them.

589
00:56:01,366 --> 00:56:09,068
What's called in the trade, mow and blow, where people walk behind lawnmowers and blow off the grass clippings.

590
00:56:11,168 --> 00:56:12,869
Some have other restrictions.

591
00:56:12,969 --> 00:56:19,990
Arlington, for example, limits the number that can be used on a lot at any one time, requires a certain amount of time off out of every hour

592
00:56:20,822 --> 00:56:24,304
But the standard thing in the area has been seasonal limitations.

593
00:56:25,985 --> 00:56:49,518
We decided to try for the whole ball of wax, and we included in ours a three and a half year phase out, which is common outside of this region, but it's not common, but there are quite a number of cities, for instance, in California that have done this, Washington DC did it, passed it three years ago, but nobody had done it in New England.

594
00:56:51,430 --> 00:57:01,015
So we included a three and a half year phase out at the end of which there could be no commercial use of backpack blowers at all.

595
00:57:01,535 --> 00:57:11,200
The sole exception was the large wheeled blowers, which you sometimes see on large properties, which are extraordinarily noisy, but they're four stroke engines.

596
00:57:12,320 --> 00:57:13,141
So they're much cleaner.

597
00:57:14,041 --> 00:57:17,423
Our bylaw allows those only on lots of an acre or more.

598
00:57:18,437 --> 00:57:21,419
it gives residents an extra year to phase out the use of theirs.

599
00:57:23,420 --> 00:57:25,801
Town meeting, flood court did not support it.

600
00:57:26,461 --> 00:57:30,523
Town meeting passed it by an overwhelming margin, 85% in favor.

601
00:57:32,124 --> 00:57:38,888
Within 12 hours, landscapers had filed a petition for a referendum to try to overturn the bylaw.

602
00:57:40,525 --> 00:57:48,428
That was resolved in March, where the town upheld the bylaw by a margin of 10%, 55 to 45%.

603
00:57:50,129 --> 00:57:56,951
So at this point, it turns out there are a number of people in a number of towns who are watching Lexington to see what would happen.

604
00:57:56,971 --> 00:58:03,814
I think sensibly enough thinking if this failed in Lexington, they might not want to try it yet in their own towns.

605
00:58:07,073 --> 00:58:14,875
a vote at town meeting in Arlington, either tonight or Wednesday, depending on how quickly they move on a similar proposal that has exactly the same phase out.

606
00:58:15,775 --> 00:58:21,736
There will be a vote in Brookline on a phase out at the end of May or early June, depending on how quickly they move.

607
00:58:21,776 --> 00:58:23,356
And there may be one in Belmont.

608
00:58:24,416 --> 00:58:36,839
And there are a number of other towns like Winchester, where people have motions in play to impose less extreme restrictions, you know, with no phase out, but with seasonal limitations and hourly limitations and so on.

609
00:58:37,766 --> 00:58:38,386
So it's spreading.

610
00:58:38,426 --> 00:58:40,428
I was actually looking, I'm at the wrong computer.

611
00:58:40,468 --> 00:58:44,130
I have a map of where these things have been proposed or enacted.

612
00:58:44,190 --> 00:58:49,193
And it's a large chunk of the Boston area now.

613
00:58:49,634 --> 00:58:55,457
Boston has hearings on a bill that would be actually more extreme than some of the town ones.

614
00:58:57,118 --> 00:58:58,699
So anyway, that's where things stand.

