A single one is 100 decibels at the ear of the operator.

642
01:01:21,616 --> 01:01:22,997
To give you an idea how bad that is,

643
01:01:24,905 --> 01:01:31,389
National Institute on Occupational Safety and Health sets a recommended exposure limit of 15 minutes per eight hour day.

644
01:01:31,409 --> 01:01:33,971
I mean, it's extraordinarily noisy.

645
01:01:34,671 --> 01:01:42,316
When you get a bunch of them together, I've measured 93 decibels in my neighborhood at a property line on several occasions.

646
01:01:42,456 --> 01:01:50,481
And to give you an idea what that means, a low altitude flyover of an F-35 is 94 decibels.

647
01:01:50,761 --> 01:01:52,242
I mean, it's extraordinarily noisy.

648
01:01:53,226 --> 01:01:54,407
So that was the primary reason.

649
01:01:54,447 --> 01:02:06,916
The other reason that had a big impact, I think, on the town vote, but it was not central politically, and I'll tell you why, is that there are very few pieces of equipment that use two-stroke engines.

650
01:02:08,257 --> 01:02:13,241
It's basically virtually all leaf blowers, chainsaws, and some string trimmers.

651
01:02:14,081 --> 01:02:17,544
The reason they use them is that the power to weight ratio is very high.

652
01:02:18,758 --> 01:02:21,420
but they are extraordinarily dirty machines.

653
01:02:22,121 --> 01:02:25,584
It's hard to overestimate or overstate how dirty they are.

654
01:02:27,465 --> 01:02:42,057
California Resources Board estimated that using one, a gas leaf blower for an hour, which is about a third of the typical usage to clear a lawn in Lexington, generates as much ozone producing emissions as driving a Toyota Camry 1,100 miles.

655
01:02:44,387 --> 01:02:48,670
And it's worse than that because the stuff that's emitted is highly toxic.

656
01:02:48,750 --> 01:02:52,353
It's PM 2.5, extremely small particulate emissions.

657
01:02:53,254 --> 01:02:56,056
A lot of volatile organic compounds that are highly toxic.

658
01:02:56,096 --> 01:02:58,118
Some are carcinogens like benzene.

659
01:02:58,818 --> 01:03:00,400
They're extraordinarily dangerous.

660
01:03:02,141 --> 01:03:11,028
That wasn't as central politically because there's some argument about whether towns have the prerogative to impose environmental restrictions on their own.

661
01:03:12,450 --> 01:03:18,152
So I think it had a big influence on how people voted, but in terms of our proposal, it was secondary.

662
01:03:19,693 --> 01:03:23,774
It was, however, I think this made a big difference politically.

663
01:03:25,415 --> 01:03:32,517
It was the reason why, or one of two reasons why, our Board of Health voted unanimously to support this bylaw.

664
01:03:34,005 --> 01:03:47,231
and they were unwilling to take a stand about residents because they said there just aren't good data about exposure levels, but they issued a statement saying that both the emissions and the noise pose a clear health risk for workers.

665
01:03:49,432 --> 01:04:02,338
So we figured we'll take on the one that is, in a sense, I don't wanna say politically easier because it was not politically easy, but it was less politically difficult than dealing with lawnmowers, for example.

666
01:04:03,922 --> 01:04:09,225
And it was the most offensive of the machines that are currently used and the most dangerous of the machines that were used.

667
01:04:12,406 --> 01:04:21,830
We did, I should say, there was a misunderstanding about an earlier version of the motion that led people to think that it would ban lawnmowers.

668
01:04:21,990 --> 01:04:24,071
And there was a firestorm of protests.

669
01:04:24,551 --> 01:04:26,852
I mean, it really generated an enormous fuss.

670
01:04:31,714 --> 01:04:32,935
Dan, this is Bob Payne.

671
01:04:32,975 --> 01:04:42,620
What are the arguments on the side of the operators or the commercial vendors to oppose such an ordinance?

672
01:04:43,401 --> 01:04:46,062
Well, they had several and some were valid.

673
01:04:47,703 --> 01:04:48,583
Some, I think, were not.

674
01:04:48,623 --> 01:04:51,505
I mean, for instance, one of the big things that they pushed toward the end was that

675
01:04:52,666 --> 01:04:58,229
They pointed out accurately enough that lithium mining is in most cases environmentally destructive.

676
01:04:58,289 --> 01:05:01,190
But I mean, the extraction of petroleum is environmentally destructive.

677
01:05:01,210 --> 01:05:03,031
That's not really the issue.

678
01:05:04,012 --> 01:05:09,134
But it's a serious disruption to their business for two reasons.

679
01:05:09,695 --> 01:05:12,196
One is that the electric machines are less powerful.

680
01:05:13,717 --> 01:05:16,658
And they would argue that it's not feasible to use them.

681
01:05:16,678 --> 01:05:17,779
That's simply untrue.

682
01:05:19,205 --> 01:05:26,987
Landscape companies that have been using electric blowers for a decade and making a profit, but you have to work differently because machines are less powerful.

683
01:05:27,047 --> 01:05:40,669
So for example, the person who does my yard, who has never owned it, it's been in business eight years and has never owned a gas blower, said that a large lot, his crews bring the leaves into smaller piles and collect from that rather than blowing across an entire property.

684
01:05:40,709 --> 01:05:43,270
So it means a change in how they work.

685
01:05:44,390 --> 01:05:48,811
It does mean for large, heavily treed properties, an increase in costs.

686
01:05:49,815 --> 01:06:00,020
The best estimates we were able to get from people with experience is that it's not uncommon for people with large properties to have a 10 or 20% increase in the cost of clearing leaves.

687
01:06:01,021 --> 01:06:08,565
The landscapers here claimed that it would triple costs, but the landscapers who were actually doing it with electric equipment said that's absolute garbage, it's nonsense.

688
01:06:09,625 --> 01:06:18,910
The other argument which really carried a lot of weight is that when you use gas leaf blowers, you spread costs fairly evenly over time.

689
01:06:19,797 --> 01:06:21,557
The blowers are not terribly expensive.

690
01:06:22,497 --> 01:06:27,258
The maintenance is fairly expensive and the fuel is expensive, but that's all spread out over time.

691
01:06:28,779 --> 01:06:33,099
The economic model with electric lawn equipment is very different.

692
01:06:33,139 --> 01:06:46,842
You pay a lot up front, particularly because of batteries, and you pay much less over time because the energy costs are lower and there's virtually no maintenance, except in unusual circumstances.

693
01:06:47,873 --> 01:06:50,837
So they face a substantial upfront cost.

694
01:06:54,081 --> 01:07:01,270
They often presented that as if there were, in fact, they invariably presented it as if there were no offsetting long-term costs, savings.

695
01:07:01,831 --> 01:07:04,254
They would simply say, oh, look, we have to spend all this money for batteries.

696
01:07:05,115 --> 01:07:06,617
And they would simply neglect to mention

697
01:07:07,758 --> 01:07:12,180
that their costs over three or four or five years were actually fairly similar.

698
01:07:12,700 --> 01:07:14,141
So we went to an organization.

699
01:07:14,221 --> 01:07:16,002
If you embark on this, you may find them useful.

700
01:07:16,022 --> 01:07:31,849
There's an organization, it's a for-profit company called AGZA, the American Green Zone Alliance, which makes its money helping jurisdictions and institutions like universities convert from gas to electric landscaping equipment, not just leaf blowers.

701
01:07:32,589 --> 01:07:37,531
It's run by a guy who started his career running a gas-powered leaf landscaping company.

702
01:07:39,849 --> 01:07:51,056
They have a lot of data that's from California, which we were unwilling to use because the leaf loads are much lighter in California, but they more recently have been generating data in the northern Midwest where leaf loads are heavy.

703
01:07:52,157 --> 01:08:02,804
So we persuaded them to give us estimates of four year costs of transitioning a three blower crew.

704
01:08:05,602 --> 01:08:06,963
They gave us quite a range.

705
01:08:07,023 --> 01:08:11,046
It depends on how much you use them, how often you replace them, what kind of oil you use.

706
01:08:11,146 --> 01:08:12,307
That actually makes a big difference.

707
01:08:13,288 --> 01:08:21,254
But their estimate of the four-year cost for crew ranged from $0 to $1,250 a year.

708
01:08:22,835 --> 01:08:30,281
And the $1,250 sounds like a lot, but if you divide that by the 500 or 600 jobs a crew does over the course of a cleanup, it's $2 or $3 a job.

709
01:08:33,203 --> 01:08:41,949
So in terms of operating expenses long-term, it's not a big deal, but it does mean they have to lay out money up front to buy the equipment.

710
01:08:41,989 --> 01:08:43,990
So our response to that was two things.

711
01:08:44,050 --> 01:08:49,374
First, we said, tell us, we said to landscapers, tell us how long you actually use the equipment.

712
01:08:50,735 --> 01:08:55,238
And it varies, but most of them keep lowers two to four years, right?

713
01:08:55,298 --> 01:08:57,359
So we said, let's make a three and a half year transition.

714
01:08:57,839 --> 01:09:01,622
You can gradually buy these machines as your old machines are run down.

715
01:09:02,512 --> 01:09:07,254
There were a lot of residents who were very displeased, who said, I don't want to listen to this stuff for three and a half more years.

716
01:09:07,874 --> 01:09:09,055
But that was our compromise.

717
01:09:09,775 --> 01:09:12,576
There are also efforts underway to cushion it.

718
01:09:12,776 --> 01:09:25,421
There is a bill before the House in Beacon Hill that would let towns set up programs to provide support for the initial purchase.

719
01:09:26,762 --> 01:09:28,123
There's actually an effort by

720
01:09:29,785 --> 01:09:34,046
Catherine's Clark's office to get an appropriation for that for her district.

721
01:09:35,046 --> 01:09:41,208
And there is a lot of interest in, and I think this is what will probably fly in long-term, bulk purchasing arrangements.

722
01:09:44,689 --> 01:09:53,231
When we started calling, my committee started calling commercial vendors and just off the top, they would say, all right, we'll give you 15%.

723
01:09:54,692 --> 01:09:59,173
But I think if you got three or four towns together, which would be three towns would probably be

724
01:10:00,493 --> 01:10:07,498
60, 70 landscapers, you could probably negotiate a really substantial bulk discount.

725
01:10:07,618 --> 01:10:10,099
But for now, anyway, those are the big arguments.

726
01:10:10,179 --> 01:10:16,223
The total costs, which they exaggerated, the initial costs, which I think were a real concern.

727
01:10:18,384 --> 01:10:27,070
They also made a strong pitch in Lexington, which I think was a mistake on their part to cast this as an issue of property rights.

728
01:10:28,897 --> 01:10:32,600
And the immediate response to that was, what about the rights of the property owners next door?

729
01:10:33,921 --> 01:10:36,163
You know, who really don't want to listen to 90 decibels.

730
01:10:37,945 --> 01:10:39,846
But I think that was the big, big arguments were.

731
01:10:39,926 --> 01:10:46,752
And if you go ahead, I have a pile of their materials, which I'd be happy to send on if you decide to go ahead.

732
01:10:46,772 --> 01:10:51,376
Because from what the people in Arlington have told me, it's exactly the same arguments there that we had.

733
01:10:54,999 --> 01:10:56,040
Barry, you have your hand up.

734
01:11:00,292 --> 01:11:02,713
And yes, so I think Luke had his up before.

735
01:11:02,833 --> 01:11:03,193
I'm sorry.

736
01:11:03,213 --> 01:11:03,573
Okay.

737
01:11:03,633 --> 01:11:04,113
I'm sorry.

738
01:11:04,733 --> 01:11:05,494
Luke, you can go ahead.

739
01:11:05,514 --> 01:11:05,954
Thank you.

740
01:11:06,234 --> 01:11:06,974
No, that's fine.

741
01:11:07,074 --> 01:11:07,454
Thank you.

742
01:11:07,974 --> 01:11:09,855
Um, so I, forgive me.

743
01:11:09,875 --> 01:11:10,915
I have two questions.

744
01:11:11,095 --> 01:11:17,117
One is, uh, uh, is the ban a gas ban or is it a two stroke engine ban?

745
01:11:17,137 --> 01:11:27,580
Um, so in the end are, uh, is it requiring an electric system or is it requiring just a quieter system?

746
01:11:28,579 --> 01:11:39,067
That's a superb question because a lot of jurisdictions have tried not banning gas leaf blowers entirely and trying to control what kind of leaf blowers are used.

747
01:11:40,869 --> 01:11:45,532
Our conversations with the people in jurisdictions that have tried that suggest that it just doesn't work.

748
01:11:46,973 --> 01:11:55,140
First of all, the four-stroke gas-powered leaf blowers tend to be less powerful because of the unfavorable power to weight ratio.

749
01:11:55,180 --> 01:11:56,401
So it's not what they want anyway.

750
01:11:57,270 --> 01:12:08,575
What a lot of towns around here have done, Newton has done at Cambridge, I think Brookline, is to impose a 65 decibel sound limit.

751
01:12:09,115 --> 01:12:15,417
The problem with that is you have to have somebody go and measure it, right?

752
01:12:15,497 --> 01:12:26,342
And so what some landscapers admitted, frankly, is that when they're working in Cambridge, if they see somebody they're worried about, they throttle down for a minute, and then when they leave, they just go back to work.

753
01:12:27,023 --> 01:12:35,851
But our thing about this was really shaped a lot by our conversation with our chief of police, who in this town is the person who's going to have to enforce this.

754
01:12:37,252 --> 01:12:38,954
And he said, it's got to be simple.

755
01:12:40,015 --> 01:12:46,500
He said, if I get a call that somebody's doing house construction at 630 in the morning, which is illegal, it's simple.

756
01:12:46,561 --> 01:12:47,581
I send somebody out.

757
01:12:47,641 --> 01:12:48,302
They say, it's 630.

758
01:12:48,382 --> 01:12:49,183
Here's your ticket.

759
01:12:50,517 --> 01:12:52,599
And he said, if you can make it that simple, we can do it.

760
01:12:52,639 --> 01:12:57,884
But he said, they're not going to go out with anti-certified meters and measure 50 feet and so on.

761
01:12:59,085 --> 01:13:05,570
So we decided the only thing that made it enforceable is to just ban them entirely.

762
01:13:06,271 --> 01:13:13,017
We spoke to people in some towns that said they actually no longer try to enforce the other limits because it just is such a headache.

763
01:13:13,740 --> 01:13:14,601
But banning what?

764
01:13:14,681 --> 01:13:16,822
Banning leaf blowers, period, or?

765
01:13:16,902 --> 01:13:19,844
We just ban all gas-powered leaf blowers.

766
01:13:20,224 --> 01:13:21,224
You can still use electric.

767
01:13:21,845 --> 01:13:26,888
You will face, if you do this, by the way, a small but vocal group of people who want all blowers banned.

768
01:13:27,248 --> 01:13:27,488
Yeah.

769
01:13:28,729 --> 01:13:29,809
I can imagine.

770
01:13:30,250 --> 01:13:33,672
Electric blowers also make plenty of noise, too.

771
01:13:33,692 --> 01:13:41,676
Well, they make a huge amount less, but they do damage overwintering pollinators, which became an issue here.

772
01:13:43,628 --> 01:13:53,214
What I said to the other people in the, we had some people in our referendum campaign who were concerned about that, some who weren't.

773
01:13:53,274 --> 01:13:55,375
And what I suggested is forget about it.

774
01:13:56,095 --> 01:14:01,839
I actually am concerned about it, but we're not gonna win this referendum if we tell people they can't use any blowers.

775
01:14:01,939 --> 01:14:04,040
It's just not politically feasible now.

776
01:14:05,001 --> 01:14:07,022
But in terms of noise, there's a huge difference.

777
01:14:07,902 --> 01:14:09,283
We created some charts.

778
01:14:09,303 --> 01:14:13,625
We had the advantage of having an acoustical consultant from MIT working with us.

779
01:14:14,266 --> 01:14:16,167
So we were able to generate numbers pretty quickly.

780
01:14:16,207 --> 01:14:31,095
But one of our charts, which he created, compared four commercial grade electric leaf blower battery powered backpack leaf blowers to one standard commercial grade gas leaf power blower.

781
01:14:31,175 --> 01:14:36,318
And the answer is the single gas leaf powered blower generated twice the noise of four electric blowers.

782
01:14:37,430 --> 01:14:48,522
But the other thing, and tell me if I'm getting into things that are too technical, all of these other numbers you will encounter use what is called the A-weighted decibel scale.

783
01:14:49,183 --> 01:14:53,608
And this is a scale that's weighted to emphasize the frequencies that are most noticeable in human hearing.

784
01:14:55,129 --> 01:14:58,473
What that scale does not emphasize is low frequencies.

785
01:14:59,958 --> 01:15:08,240
And the real difference apart from overall noise levels between gas-powered leaf blowers and electric blowers is gas-powered leaf blowers produce a lot of low-frequency noise.

786
01:15:09,440 --> 01:15:11,140
And the impact of that is twofold.

787
01:15:11,260 --> 01:15:16,121
One is it travels much further, and the other is it penetrates surfaces much better.

788
01:15:17,102 --> 01:15:23,943
So when you have somebody two doors down from you doing this and you close the windows and it's still annoying, that's why it's the low-frequency noise.

789
01:15:25,199 --> 01:15:32,324
So, I mean, our acoustical experts said he thought all of the numbers we were using understated the problem because they were using the wrong scale.

790
01:15:34,566 --> 01:15:40,130
Well, and you answered in part my second question, but I do just want to get it out there.

791
01:15:40,210 --> 01:15:41,851
And that is in terms of enforcement.

792
01:15:42,792 --> 01:15:51,038
So this will be enforced by the police department and basically a neighbor can report and see what.

793
01:15:51,518 --> 01:15:51,818
Yes.

794
01:15:53,779 --> 01:15:56,481
The other towns that have done this have done all sorts of things.

795
01:15:56,541 --> 01:16:03,246
I mean, in our case, it's partly because we're small enough that we don't have some of the agencies that, say, Cambridge has.

796
01:16:05,508 --> 01:16:11,373
So legally, we have four agencies that have authority to enforce noise ordinances.

797
01:16:11,473 --> 01:16:19,739
It's the building commissioner, who's clearly not going to do this, the fire department, which is clearly not going to do this, the police, and the health department.

798
01:16:20,980 --> 01:16:23,362
So in practice, what this boils down to

799
01:16:24,258 --> 01:16:28,640
is that when there is persistent noise, that can be measured.

800
01:16:29,781 --> 01:16:45,927
So from ongoing breaking of rock and construction, for example, the health department takes it because they actually have staff who can go out with ANSI certified meters and take the measurements that are required under state law for the standard state standard, which is a 10 decibel increment.

801
01:16:46,568 --> 01:16:50,589
But they have no way of going out there when the landscaper is next door to me for 45 minutes.

802
01:16:52,713 --> 01:16:53,674
It had to be the police.

803
01:16:55,935 --> 01:16:56,255
Thank you.

804
01:16:57,255 --> 01:16:58,096
OK, let's see.

805
01:16:58,116 --> 01:16:59,016
Let's go to Barry.

806
01:16:59,256 --> 01:16:59,897
Your questions.

807
01:17:01,157 --> 01:17:02,898
Yeah, mine's simpler.

808
01:17:05,439 --> 01:17:07,540
Can you attach numbers to the cost?

809
01:17:07,820 --> 01:17:14,324
What does a typical electric leaf blower cost up front?

810
01:17:15,459 --> 01:17:29,748
And how does that compare to, say, monthly revenues for a single crew, which I imagine a crew would have one leaf blower, right?

811
01:17:30,209 --> 01:17:30,749
Well, no.

812
01:17:30,789 --> 01:17:33,150
Around here, the crews often have three to five.

813
01:17:33,891 --> 01:17:37,793
Oh, so there's a whole bunch of people in the crew, and each one has a leaf blower.

814
01:17:37,974 --> 01:17:40,375
So I don't have those numbers off the top of my head.

815
01:17:40,475 --> 01:17:42,396
I'd be happy to get them to you.

816
01:17:42,537 --> 01:17:44,818
Email me, and I'll send you a page that has them.

817
01:17:48,498 --> 01:17:51,080
It's a low entry barrier field, right?

818
01:17:51,180 --> 01:17:55,344
So the way most of this works is people go in and finance equipment.

819
01:17:55,484 --> 01:17:57,867
I mean, leaf blowers are not their expensive equipment.

820
01:17:57,887 --> 01:18:00,929
The expensive equipment are their trucks, their trailers, right?

821
01:18:02,971 --> 01:18:06,114
So would some of them have to finance purchases?

822
01:18:06,194 --> 01:18:06,595
Probably.

823
01:18:07,392 --> 01:18:07,992
Probably so.

824
01:18:08,012 --> 01:18:10,034
OK, that's what I'm getting at.

825
01:18:10,054 --> 01:18:23,442
It just seems like, you know, amounts of money that might sound like it's an expensive piece of equipment compared to the overall cost of running the business might be inconsequential or it might not be.

826
01:18:23,602 --> 01:18:25,263
I mean, that's why I'm asking for the numbers.

827
01:18:25,783 --> 01:18:27,064
No, it actually is.

828
01:18:27,624 --> 01:18:30,646
The real cost of of clearing leaves are two things.

829
01:18:30,706 --> 01:18:33,728
I mean, the great bulk of the cost is labor.

830
01:18:35,108 --> 01:18:35,729
And tipping fees.

831
01:18:36,704 --> 01:18:39,627
In most of this area, it costs a lot of money to dump the leaves.

832
01:18:40,988 --> 01:18:49,915
And the equipment costs, even the expensive equipment costs, when you average it over lots and lots of jobs, things like big trucks, are not what most of the bill is.

833
01:18:50,456 --> 01:18:57,321
There was a landscaper in Lexington, he actually quit Lexington when this bill passed, who used to provide bills that broke it down.

834
01:18:58,462 --> 01:19:03,667
And one of our select board members used his service, and close to half of the bill was tipping fees.

835
01:19:04,963 --> 01:19:11,508
So this is not a major portion of their expense, but it is a major outlay initially.

836
01:19:11,648 --> 01:19:15,010
I mean, and it's not, again, it's not the blowers for the most part.

837
01:19:15,630 --> 01:19:17,852
There's one company where that's an exception.

838
01:19:17,912 --> 01:19:18,633
It's the batteries.

839
01:19:19,994 --> 01:19:22,455
You need a lot of batteries to get through a day.

840
01:19:22,495 --> 01:19:33,323
So the most experienced electric landscaper in this area, this guy in Concord, who said he sends out a crew with three blowers

841
01:19:34,278 --> 01:19:35,319
with 24 batteries.

842
01:19:37,740 --> 01:19:46,704
And he said when he converted, when he purchased the equipment, he said the bulk of his expense, other than things like trucks, was batteries, wasn't chargers.

843
01:19:47,024 --> 01:19:48,625
It wasn't blowers, it was batteries.

844
01:19:49,605 --> 01:20:03,352
There are some people, not here, but in the Washington DC area, there are some landscapers who handle this differently and put in their contracts with customers that they can charge batteries at a customer's house for the next job.

845
01:20:04,633 --> 01:20:06,374
